{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And YHWH rained.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory mentioned three opinions concerning this verse, and thus they expounded: Rabbi Yehudah bar Simon said, ״וה' המטיר״ — this is Gabriel; ״מאת ה'״ — this is the Holy One, blessed be He. Rabbi Eliezer said: In every place where it says ״וה'״, it is He and His court. Rabbi Yitzhak said: In the Torah, in the Prophets, and in the Writings, we find that an ordinary person mentions his name twice — in the Torah: ״ויאמר למך לנשיו״ and ״נשי למך״ — thus far in the Midrash. You thus have three opinions in explaining the verse: for Rabbi Yehudah attributed the first name to Gabriel, for he is the emissary sent to destroy, and the emissary is called by the name of the sender. And Rabbi Eliezer says that He and His court agreed in judgment, and from Him it came. And Rabbi Yitzhak says that it is the way of the language." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, the opinion of Rabbi Eliezer is the correct one. And they stated a great principle: every place where it says ״וה'״, it is He and His court." "<b>And you should</b> reflect, for His court is that concerning which is said (Genesis 31:13) ״אנכי האל בית אל״, and this is what Yonatan ben Uzziel translated: ״עילאה בית שכנתיה״ — He mentioned the expression of בית and the expression of שכינה, and concerning it was said of Moses our master, peace be upon him (Numbers 12:7) ״בכל ביתי נאמן הוא״. And it is the emanated essence, separated from the foundation of the world, as the Ramban, of blessed memory, mentioned in his commentary on the verse ״בכל״, and it is what is said (Exodus 13:21) ״וה' הולך לפניהם יומם״, and adjacent to it ״ויסע מלאך האלהים״, and it is adjacent only by the light. And thus He mentioned above ״וה' אמר המכסה אני מאברהם״, and He said ״ושמרו דרך ה'״, and did not say ״דרכי״. And ״ולמען הביא ה'״, and did not say ״אביא״. And so in every place where you find the special name with the addition of the letter vav, the intent is the addition of His court, and the root of them all is ״וה' המטיר״. And you already know that it is the expression of pouring down." "<b>From YHWH out of heaven.</b> It is the great name, as He said ״ואתה תשמע השמים״, for from Him comes the punishment; therefore the expression of ״ארדה נא ואראה״ is mentioned concerning it, in the manner of the generation of the dispersion concerning which is written there (ibid. 11) ״וירד ה'״ — and understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {3 "The prooftext 'ואתה תשמע השמים' is from 1 Kings 8:32 (Solomon's prayer); cited without a reference. The 'ibid. 11' for וירד ה' refers back to Genesis 11:5, just cited implicitly through the allusion to וירד."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:46.876216106Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}